WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:13.057
On May 26, 1940, the German army cornered British and French soldiers on the beaches of Dunkirk, France.

2
00:00:14.338 --> 00:00:22.304
Over the next ten days, about 338,000 desperate soldiers were rescued and evacuated across the English Channel to

3
00:00:22.344 --> 00:00:27.368
Britain by hundreds of civilian and military boats, to escape the German army.

4
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.522
Winston Churchill had only been prime minister of England for about a month when he spoke to the

5
00:00:33.562 --> 00:00:41.686
House of Commons on June 4, 1940, after the retreat at Dunkirk: "We shall defend our island, whatever

6
00:00:41.726 --> 00:00:47.968
the cost may be, we shall fight on the beaches We shall fight in the fields and in

7
00:00:48.008 --> 00:00:53.050
the streets, we shall fight in the hills; we shall never surrender."

8
00:00:54.091 --> 00:00:59.521
Through the years of hardship to come during World War II, the British people would turn to Churchill

9
00:00:59.741 --> 00:01:02.702
and his inspiring words again and again.

10
00:01:04.203 --> 00:01:12.687
Churchill was born in England on November 30, 1874, to Lord Randolph Churchill and Jennie Jerome, an American

11
00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:20.090
heiress. Four weeks before his eighth birthday, Churchill was sent to a boarding school and remained away from

12
00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:21.731
home for most of his childhood.

13
00:01:22.671 --> 00:01:27.810
He missed his parents very much and would write letters to his mother and father begging them to

14
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:29.551
visit. They rarely did.

15
00:01:30.752 --> 00:01:35.055
Once, he read in the newspaper that his father had given a speech nearby.

16
00:01:36.016 --> 00:01:42.140
He wrote, "I cannot think why you did not come to see me I was very disappointed, but

17
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.921
I suppose you were too busy to come."

18
00:01:45.242 --> 00:01:50.726
At age 14, he was sent to Harrow, an elite public school, and it was there that his

19
00:01:50.806 --> 00:01:53.148
remarkable gift for language blossomed.

20
00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:00.224
Despite a speech impediment, a lisp that made it hard for him to speak clearly, he won a

21
00:02:00.304 --> 00:02:05.847
school competition by reciting over 1,200 lines of poetry from memory.

22
00:02:06.707 --> 00:02:12.731
But Lord Randolph did not think his son had the intelligence to go into politics, so Churchill was

23
00:02:12.791 --> 00:02:14.732
encouraged to become a soldier.

24
00:02:15.753 --> 00:02:24.557
In 1893, Churchill entered Sandhurst, the British military college; a year later, he graduated and joined the army.

25
00:02:26.038 --> 00:02:36.042
As a cavalry officer between 1895 and 1898, Churchill fought in Cuba, India, what is now northwestern Pakistan,

26
00:02:36.462 --> 00:02:37.182
and Sudan.

27
00:02:38.163 --> 00:02:45.646
He wrote about his military experiences, and after leaving the military in 1899, he wrote news reports from

28
00:02:45.726 --> 00:02:49.127
places as far flung as Egypt and South Africa.

29
00:02:49.968 --> 00:02:54.470
In South Africa, he was taken prisoner, escaping with a bounty on his head.

30
00:02:55.370 --> 00:03:03.694
His thrilling tale of capture and escape, London to Ladysmith via Pretoria, made him a popular, well-paid author.

31
00:03:05.134 --> 00:03:10.917
In 1900, he won a seat in Parliament and went on a speaking tour in the United States.,

32
00:03:11.377 --> 00:03:14.918
where his speeches revealed a powerful oratory style.

33
00:03:15.919 --> 00:03:22.182
He met the famous American writer Mark Twain, who introduced the 26-year-old Churchill to a New York audience

34
00:03:22.322 --> 00:03:26.724
as "the future prime minister of England."

35
00:03:26.864 --> 00:03:33.026
In 1911, Churchill was named first lord of the Admiralty, and when World War I broke out in

36
00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:40.470
1914, he found himself desperately looking for a way around the trench warfare stalemate on the western front.

37
00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:48.422
Churchill decided to get around the impasse by attacking the Turks, who were allied with Germany, at Constantinople

38
00:03:48.442 --> 00:03:49.863
(now Istanbul).

39
00:03:50.783 --> 00:03:57.267
Churchill sent soldiers to the Gallipoli Peninsula, on the coast of Turkey, but there was fierce resistance from

40
00:03:57.287 --> 00:03:57.727
the Turks.

41
00:03:58.727 --> 00:04:04.150
When the British soldiers landed on the beaches, the enemy had the high ground and smashed them.

42
00:04:05.091 --> 00:04:11.594
More than 250,000 troops died at Gallipoli, and Churchill's strategy was widely blamed.

43
00:04:12.515 --> 00:04:16.153
"I thought he would never get over it," his wife, Clementine, said.

44
00:04:16.173 --> 00:04:18.555
"I thought he would die of grief."

45
00:04:19.195 --> 00:04:24.439
Churchill resigned his position, but by 1917, he was back in government.

46
00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:33.826
Over the years, Churchill held different governmental posts, but he left again in 1931 to devote himself completely

47
00:04:33.866 --> 00:04:39.090
to finishing The World Crisis, a six-volume history of the First World War.

48
00:04:40.111 --> 00:04:49.227
In the early 1930s, while visiting famous World War I European battlefields, Churchill noticed uniformed youth marching in

49
00:04:49.247 --> 00:04:53.109
the streets and became convinced that Hitler was planning war.

50
00:04:54.129 --> 00:05:00.952
He began warning about the dangers posed by Nazi Germany and pointed out England's lack of military readiness,

51
00:05:01.532 --> 00:05:05.454
but others did not share his views, and he was largely ignored.

52
00:05:06.474 --> 00:05:14.162
Then, in 1938, when Germany invaded Czechoslovakia, some politicians turned to Churchill for guidance.

53
00:05:15.162 --> 00:05:22.426
But Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain still preferred to negotiate with the German dictator rather than resist him.

54
00:05:23.827 --> 00:05:31.591
Germany invaded Poland in 1939, and Britain and France declared war on Germany—World War II had begun.

55
00:05:32.572 --> 00:05:39.636
In the spring of 1940, George VI, the king of England, lost confidence in Chamberlain as prime minister

56
00:05:40.128 --> 00:05:41.569
and replaced him with Churchill.

57
00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:49.274
The task ahead was daunting: England and France were fighting together against Germany, but Churchill knew they were

58
00:05:49.314 --> 00:05:51.376
not powerful enough to defeat Hitler.

59
00:05:52.397 --> 00:05:59.401
During the war years, Churchill gave the greatest speeches of his life, inspiring the British people to stand

60
00:05:59.481 --> 00:06:03.724
strong against Nazi Germany and urging other nations to join the battle.

61
00:06:04.625 --> 00:06:08.688
"Hitler knows that he will have to break us or lose the war.

62
00:06:09.256 --> 00:06:13.918
If we can stand up to him, all Europe may be free and the life of the world

63
00:06:13.958 --> 00:06:18.940
may move forward into broad, sunlit uplands," he said in one stirring speech.

64
00:06:20.181 --> 00:06:22.982
Visiting Harrow, Churchill spoke to the students.

65
00:06:23.002 --> 00:06:25.723
"This is the lesson," he said.

66
00:06:25.743 --> 00:06:33.087
"Never give in, never give in, never, never, never."

67
00:06:34.547 --> 00:06:39.641
In the summer of 1940, the Battle of Britain was being fought in the skies over England.

68
00:06:40.621 --> 00:06:43.162
Hitler's bombs fell day and night.

69
00:06:43.762 --> 00:06:48.243
It was a terrible time for England, but British fighter pilots fought bravely.

70
00:06:48.263 --> 00:06:56.105
"Never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few," Churchill

71
00:06:56.145 --> 00:07:00.306
said of the tireless Royal Air Force.

72
00:07:00.426 --> 00:07:07.344
By 1941, Churchill was working to forge a "grand alliance" with America and the Soviet Union, countries with

73
00:07:07.424 --> 00:07:09.245
vast military resources.

74
00:07:10.345 --> 00:07:14.586
Later, he said, "If we are together, nothing is impossible.

75
00:07:15.186 --> 00:07:18.107
If we are divided, all will fail."

76
00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:26.930
By 1945, Churchill's grand alliance had triumphed, and on May 8, victory in Europe had been declared.

77
00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:34.832
The end of the war brought political turmoil as England faced the task of rebuilding, and just a

78
00:07:34.872 --> 00:07:39.934
few months later, shockingly, Churchill was defeated in a general election.

79
00:07:41.055 --> 00:07:48.699
Although he was out of office, Churchill was respected around the globe as a legendary speaker, statesman, and

80
00:07:48.839 --> 00:07:58.124
author. His books The Second World War and A History of the English-Speaking Peoples met with worldwide acclaim,

81
00:07:58.804 --> 00:08:06.288
and in 1953, Churchill won the Nobel Prize in Literature in part for The Second World War and

82
00:08:06.368 --> 00:08:08.410
his many inspiring speeches.

83
00:08:09.671 --> 00:08:18.196
In 1951, Churchill was again prime minister, but resigned in 1955 at the age of 81 due to

84
00:08:18.276 --> 00:08:19.837
weariness and poor health.

85
00:08:20.518 --> 00:08:25.981
He died on January 24, 1965, at the age of ninety.

86
00:08:27.662 --> 00:08:35.147
In 2002, the British Broadcasting Corporation asked the people of England to vote on who was the greatest

87
00:08:36.416 --> 00:08:41.938
Briton. William Shakespeare, Princess Diana, and Charles Darwin were among the candidates.

88
00:08:43.218 --> 00:08:50.700
Nearly fifty years after his death, Churchill won over a million votes and was named the greatest Briton

89
00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:51.700
of all time.

90
00:08:52.841 --> 00:08:58.262
Churchill's brilliant speeches live on today and are often quoted by world leaders.

91
00:08:59.603 --> 00:09:06.019
"I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears, and sweat," he had said in his first speech

92
00:09:06.079 --> 00:09:13.711
as prime minister, but it was exactly what England, and perhaps the entire world, needed at the time.
